================
``langid.c`` readme
================

Introduction
------------
`langid.c` is an experimental implementation of the language identifier
described by [1] in pure C. It is largely based on the design of
`langid.py`[2], and uses `langid.py` to train models. 

Planned features
----------------
See TODO

Speed
-----

Initial comparisons against Google's cld2[3] suggest that `langid.c` is about
twice as fast.

    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] wc -l wikifiles 
    28600 wikifiles
    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] time cat wikifiles | ./compact_lang_det_batch > xxx
    cat wikifiles  0.00s user 0.00s system 0% cpu 7.989 total
    ./compact_lang_det_batch > xxx  7.77s user 0.60s system 98% cpu 8.479 total
    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] time cat wikifiles | ./langidOs -b > xxx           
    cat wikifiles  0.00s user 0.00s system 0% cpu 3.577 total
    ./langidOs -b > xxx  3.44s user 0.24s system 97% cpu 3.759 total

    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] wc -l rcv2files 
    20000 rcv2files
    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] time cat rcv2files | ./langidO2 -b > xxx     
    cat rcv2files  0.00s user 0.00s system 0% cpu 31.702 total
    ./langidO2 -b > xxx  8.23s user 0.54s system 22% cpu 38.644 total
    (langid.c) @mlui langid.c git:[master] time cat rcv2files | ./compact_lang_det_batch > xxx 
    cat rcv2files  0.00s user 0.00s system 0% cpu 18.343 total
    ./compact_lang_det_batch > xxx  18.14s user 0.53s system 97% cpu 19.155 total


Model Training
--------------

Google's protocol buffers [4] are used to transfer models between languages. The
Python program `ldpy2ldc.py` can convert a model produced by langid.py [2] into
the protocol-buffer format, and also the C source format used to compile an
in-built model directly into executable.

Shell version
-------------
`langid.sh` is a port of the `langid` tool to POSIX sh + awk + od, for
systems without a C compiler. It takes the same options (`-l`, `-b`,
`-m MODEL`, interactive mode on a terminal) and gives the same answers, but is
roughly 100x slower, so it suits occasional use rather than large batches.

    echo "Bonjour tout le monde" | ./langid.sh       # fr,22
    ./langid.sh -l < sample.txt

On first use it converts the in-built model (model.c) into a text model,
ldpy.tmodel (a few seconds, one-time). Other langid.py models can be used with
`-m` after exporting them with `make acquis.tmodel` (needs Python) or
converting a model.c with `./langid.sh --convert model.c out.tmodel`.
Set `AWK=mawk` or `AWK=gawk` to use a faster awk.

Dependencies
------------
Protocol buffers [4]
protobuf-c [5]
CMake >= 3.16 (optional, for the CMake build)
Python 3 (or 2.7) with `langid` and `numpy`, only needed to regenerate models

Building
--------

    ./build.sh                  # configure + build with CMake, copies ./langid
    ./build.sh --regen-model    # regenerate model.c/model.h from ldpy.model
    ./build.sh --model my.model # compile in a different langid.py model
    ./build.sh --python         # also build the _langid Python 3 extension
    ./build.sh --debug          # debug build with -DDEBUG output

Or with CMake directly:

    cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build

The plain Makefile still works (`make`, `make PYTHON=python2`), and the
Python extension can also be built with `python3 setup.py build_ext --inplace`.

To produce a protocol-buffer model loadable at run time with `langid -m`:

    make ldpy.pmodel

